          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "Blood pressure is measured using two numbers: the systolic pressure (when the heart contracts) and the diastolic pressure (when the heart is at rest between beats). Both measurements are taken in millimeters of mercury (mmHg). Blood pressure can fluctuate throughout the day due to various factors like activity, stress, and sleep. \n\nBecause this pressure varies, healthcare providers rely on repeated measurements to diagnose hypertension. A single high reading is not sufficient for diagnosis since it may be influenced by temporary factors rather than indicating a persistent issue. This information comes from the conditions related to hypertension in the general clinical reference.\n\nI could not cover the specific techniques or devices used to measure blood pressure, such as whether manual or automated methods are preferable.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
